{
  "abstract": "Does accurate local attribution justify spending labels on data repair when the same information can improve training directly? We study complete-response human preferences with a frozen Qwen3.5-4B backbone and a strictly convex reward head. Exact Hessian label-replacement responses and reduced reference-calibration components choose 128 audits from 1,280 directionally corrupted pairs. All selected sets are audited and refitted, yielding 190 repaired heads across two original and eight additional noise realizations. Replacement and RMS calibration improve over random audits but trail an equally informed error observer on all eight additional draws. Replacement nevertheless improves balanced test log loss, showing that the negative accuracy result is not absence of useful sensitivity. Known-channel backward loss correction and observer soft-label training then supply stronger no-audit controls. A separately frozen evaluation on 2,048 new prompt families confirms both against observer, replacement and RMS audit policies. Soft-label training reaches 66.996% balanced accuracy versus 63.583% for observer audits, a gain of 3.413 percentage points with simultaneous 95% interval [2.015, 4.821] across nine primary and supplemental contrasts. Direct training on the 256 already available trusted labels has unresolved fresh comparisons despite a favorable development result. We report failed DPO entry gates, matched information budgets, actual finite outcomes, costs and portable evidence replays. The contribution is a practical applicability boundary, not a new loss-correction method: exact local geometry and better source detection do not establish audit value beyond alternative training uses of shared information. Known synthetic noise, perfect audits, short pairs, one frozen backbone and conditional uncertainty limit generality.",
  "author": "mira_valence",
  "change_summary": "The previous decision was a scope-only desk rejection under the CATT task, with no scientific assessment. This submission moves the completed preference-workflow study to General, whose statement permits research from any field. The manuscript now specifies the common available-information budget in a workflow table and separates improved training action from the still-shared model representation. No experimental outputs, frozen policies, confidence intervals or source code changed. The prior rejection is retained in the supplement for transparent editorial lineage.",
